WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.470
Film Courage: What is a popular film that
script breaks all the screenwriting rules

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:06.400
and yet is still a success?

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:11.960
How often does this happen and is this something
that bothers you in any way?

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:19.540
John Truby: That’s an interesting question
and you really have to go to that concept

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.580
of screenwriting rules.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:32.059
And in my Anatomy of Story classes I talk
about all of the fundamental key elements

00:00:32.059 --> 00:00:34.980
to be in your script for it to be a great
story.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:40.519
We are literally talking about how you tell
a great story.

00:00:40.519 --> 00:00:49.160
In that sense there is no popular film that
I can tell you that breaks those rules.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:54.720
Because we’re talking about two different
concepts of what that rule means.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.449
Most people think about the Rules of Writing.

00:00:57.449 --> 00:01:05.230
They are really talking about these very conventional
type of ideas, about what makes a good script.

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:11.280
I’m not interested in that because that
is mechanical writing or that is paint-by-numbers

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:12.330
script writing.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.360
And that simply won’t work.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:21.250
When I talk about the rules of good story,
I’m talking about key elements way under

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:28.310
the surface that link a story together so
it builds steadily from beginning to end.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:36.920
Now at that level of story, what I call the
Grammar of Drama, a story can build so that

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.840
it has the greatest impact on the audience.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:47.100
There is no way to write a great script that
doesn’t hit those beats.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:56.070
Now, here is the qualify which is when you
transcend a genre, when you use (for example)

00:01:56.070 --> 00:02:03.900
advanced storytelling techniques you come
up with a story that appears to break those

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:05.070
basic rules.

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.570
But it doesn’t.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:13.180
What it’s simply doing is coming up with
characters and story structures that are complex,

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:18.499
they are not the simple chronological beginning-to-end
type of story.

00:02:18.499 --> 00:02:25.080
And these stories might have a main character
who is “unlikable.”

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:30.730
That’s one of those conventional rules that
I mentioned that you hear when you’re first

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:33.549
starting out as a writer “Well I have to
have a likable hero.”

00:02:33.549 --> 00:02:38.740
No…that is false.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:45.650
So it is very important for writers to understand
what these “rules” are that make a great

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:52.349
story and these so called conventional rules
that are not true.

00:02:52.349 --> 00:02:55.580
They are certainly not true now and they probably
never were.

